    "topic_title": "Logarithmic Function Context and Data Modeling",
    "stem": "A training program models a learner’s improvement in score, in points, after t hours of practice by G(t) = 10 log₂(t), for t ≥ 1. Which statement correctly interprets the model at t = 8 and describes its predicted gains over successive one-hour intervals?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "After 8 hours, the predicted score improvement is 30 points, and successive one-hour intervals produce larger gains.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Increasing-versus-accelerating confusion: an increasing output does not necessarily mean increasing gains. This logarithmic model predicts smaller gains over successive equal time intervals. Fix: Compare G(2) − G(1) with G(3) − G(2) before describing the gains."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "After 8 hours, the predicted score improvement is 30 points, and successive one-hour intervals produce smaller gains.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Since 2³ = 8, G(8) = 10 · 3 = 30 points. This logarithmic model increases, but the additional score improvement over each successive one-hour interval gets smaller."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "After 8 hours, the predicted score improvement is 40 points, and successive one-hour intervals produce smaller gains.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Logarithm-as-division error: log₂(8) is not 8/2. It is the exponent that makes 2 raised to that power equal 8, so its value is 3. Fix: Find the exponent in 2 raised to a power that equals 8."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "After 8 hours, the predicted score improvement is 3 points, and successive one-hour intervals produce smaller gains.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Omitted-coefficient error: 3 is the value of log₂(8), not the model’s output. The coefficient 10 scales that value to a predicted improvement of 30 points. Fix: Multiply the logarithm’s value by the model’s coefficient before reporting points."
     }
    ]
   },
