   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-precalculus",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.14",
    "topic_title": "Logarithmic Function Context and Data Modeling",
    "stem": "A plant's height h, in centimeters, is measured t weeks after planting. The data are: t = 1, h = 10; t = 2, h = 13; t = 4, h = 16; t = 8, h = 19; t = 16, h = 22. Which statement gives an appropriate model type for h as a function of t, together with a correct justification based on the data?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Logarithmic, because each time the input doubles, the output increases by the same amount, 3 centimeters.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Inputs that grow by a constant factor paired with outputs that grow by a constant difference is the signature of a logarithmic model, here h = 10 + 3·log₂(t). The average rates of change, 3, 1.5, 0.75 and 0.375 cm per week, shrink, matching fast-then-slowing growth."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Linear, because the output increases by 3 centimeters from each row of the table to the next.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap ignores the unequal spacing of the inputs. The increase of 3 cm occurs over 1, 2, 4 and 8 weeks, so the rate of change is not constant and the data are not linear. Fix: Divide each output change by the actual input change before calling data linear."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Exponential, because consecutive output values have a common ratio.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap confuses the doubling inputs with exponential outputs. The output ratios are 13/10 = 1.3 and 16/13 ≈ 1.23, which are not equal, so there is no common ratio. Fix: Compute at least two consecutive output ratios and check whether they match."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Exponential, because the plant's rate of growth speeds up as time goes on.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the common error of picking a model without checking whether growth accelerates or slows. The average rates of change are 3, 1.5, 0.75 and 0.375 cm per week, so growth is slowing, which is logarithmic behavior. Fix: Compute the average rate of change on each interval and ask whether it grows or shrinks."
     }
    ]
   },
